{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a9d0ff26132faef4d","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01LST5ZJ8KaiCtMLFsQHwsix","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-iwlyVP/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-translate-iwlyVP-translated-rust/e867ece2-5670-426a-953e-de496239b763/tasks/a9d0ff26132faef4d.output","summary":"Done. `src/jsdtoa.rs` is written and verified.\n\n## What was written\n\n**File:** `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-iwlyVP/translated_rust/src/jsdtoa.rs` — **842 lines**. No other file was touched.\n\n**Exported `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C-unwind\"` symbols:**\n- `js_fmtexp(p: *mut c_char, e: c_int)`\n- `js_grisu2(v: f64, buffer: *mut c_char, K: *mut c_int) -> c_int`\n- `js_strtod(string: *const c_char, endPtr: *mut *mut c_char) -> f64`\n\n**Private (were `static` in C):** `cached_power`, `k_comp`, `minus`, `multiply`, `double_to_uint64`, `double2diy_fp`, `normalize_boundary`, `normalized_boundaries`, `digit_gen`, plus `struct diy_fp_t` (with a `pub type diy_fp = diy_fp_t;` alias) and the statics `powers_ten` (687 × `u64`), `powers_ten_e` (687 × `c_int`), `maxExponent`, `powersOf10`.\n\n## Translation notes\n\n- Both lookup tables were extracted from `c_src/src/jsdtoa.c` programmatically (687 entries each, format-validated) rather than transcribed by hand.\n- `double_to_uint64` keeps the literal `memcpy(&n, &d, 8)` form for exact C semantics.\n- All 64-bit arithmetic in `multiply`/`digit_gen`/`minus`/`normalized_boundaries` uses `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_sub`/`wrapping_mul` and `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` (the latter masks the shift count like x86 `shl`, matching gcc's behaviour on the shift counts C leaves UB).\n- `k_comp` uses `cvt_i32(ceil(...))` for the `double`→`int` cast.\n- `errno = ERANGE` is done via a locally declared `extern \"C\" { fn __errno_location() -> *mut c_int; }` and `const ERANGE: c_int = 34;` (nothing added to `src/cstd.rs`).\n- `goto done` in `js_strtod` became a `'done: { ... break 'done; ... }` labelled block.\n- `js_fmtexp` uses raw-pointer access into its `char se[9]` scratch buffer instead of indexed access, so it cannot panic on the out-of-range exponents where the original C code overruns that buffer (see caveat below).\n\n## Verification\n\n`cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep 'jsdtoa\\.rs'` → **empty**; zero errors and zero warnings attributable to this file. (The crate still fails overall due to not-yet-written modules — errors are confined to `lib.rs` module resolution plus `jsrun.rs`/`jserror.rs`/`jsproperty.rs`.) I confirmed the module really is type-checked by compiling `cstd.rs` + `jsi.rs` + `jsdtoa.rs` as a standalone crate.\n\nI additionally built a differential test linking the real C object (exports renamed via `-Djs_grisu2=c_js_grisu2` etc.) against a Rust staticlib and compared results byte-for-byte: **5,098,724 checks, 0 mismatches**, covering `js_fmtexp` for every exponent in [-100000, 100000]; `js_grisu2` over 3M random bit patterns plus 300K scaled decimals and hand-picked edge cases (denormals, `5e-324`, `DBL_MAX`, powers of ten); and `js_strtod` over ~80 hand-picked strings plus 800K random strings, comparing the returned double's raw bits, the `endPtr` offset, and `errno` (including the `ERANGE` paths for `1e512` / `1e-512`).\n\nOne caveat worth flagging, not a defect I introduced: the C `js_fmtexp` writes into a 9-byte `char se[9]` and overruns it for exponents needing 10 digits (e.g. `INT_MAX`), which segfaulted my harness when I probed it. The Rust port reproduces the same out-of-bounds write rather than diverging, per the \"do not fix bugs\" rule; real callers only pass exponents up to about ±330.","usage":{"total_tokens":103286,"tool_uses":22,"duration_ms":363766},"uuid":"749043cb-4713-45ea-8fc5-96daf8bf42b8","session_id":"e867ece2-5670-426a-953e-de496239b763"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01LST5ZJ8KaiCtMLFsQHwsix","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `src/jsdtoa.rs` is written and verified.\n\n## What was written\n\n**File:** `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-iwlyVP/translated_rust/src/jsdtoa.rs` — **842 lines**. No other file was touched.\n\n**Exported `#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C-unwind\"` symbols:**\n- `js_fmtexp(p: *mut c_char, e: c_int)`\n- `js_grisu2(v: f64, buffer: *mut c_char, K: *mut c_int) -> c_int`\n- `js_strtod(string: *const c_char, endPtr: *mut *mut c_char) -> f64`\n\n**Private (were `static` in C):** `cached_power`, `k_comp`, `minus`, `multiply`, `double_to_uint64`, `double2diy_fp`, `normalize_boundary`, `normalized_boundaries`, `digit_gen`, plus `struct diy_fp_t` (with a `pub type diy_fp = diy_fp_t;` alias) and the statics `powers_ten` (687 × `u64`), `powers_ten_e` (687 × `c_int`), `maxExponent`, `powersOf10`.\n\n## Translation notes\n\n- Both lookup tables were extracted from `c_src/src/jsdtoa.c` programmatically (687 entries each, format-validated) rather than transcribed by hand.\n- `double_to_uint64` keeps the literal `memcpy(&n, &d, 8)` form for exact C semantics.\n- All 64-bit arithmetic in `multiply`/`digit_gen`/`minus`/`normalized_boundaries` uses `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_sub`/`wrapping_mul` and `wrapping_shl`/`wrapping_shr` (the latter masks the shift count like x86 `shl`, matching gcc's behaviour on the shift counts C leaves UB).\n- `k_comp` uses `cvt_i32(ceil(...))` for the `double`→`int` cast.\n- `errno = ERANGE` is done via a locally declared `extern \"C\" { fn __errno_location() -> *mut c_int; }` and `const ERANGE: c_int = 34;` (nothing added to `src/cstd.rs`).\n- `goto done` in `js_strtod` became a `'done: { ... break 'done; ... }` labelled block.\n- `js_fmtexp` uses raw-pointer access into its `char se[9]` scratch buffer instead of indexed access, so it cannot panic on the out-of-range exponents where the original C code overruns that buffer (see caveat below).\n\n## Verification\n\n`cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep 'jsdtoa\\.rs'` → **empty**; zero errors and zero warnings attributable to this file. (The crate still fails overall due to not-yet-written modules — errors are confined to `lib.rs` module resolution plus `jsrun.rs`/`jserror.rs`/`jsproperty.rs`.) I confirmed the module really is type-checked by compiling `cstd.rs` + `jsi.rs` + `jsdtoa.rs` as a standalone crate.\n\nI additionally built a differential test linking the real C object (exports renamed via `-Djs_grisu2=c_js_grisu2` etc.) against a Rust staticlib and compared results byte-for-byte: **5,098,724 checks, 0 mismatches**, covering `js_fmtexp` for every exponent in [-100000, 100000]; `js_grisu2` over 3M random bit patterns plus 300K scaled decimals and hand-picked edge cases (denormals, `5e-324`, `DBL_MAX`, powers of ten); and `js_strtod` over ~80 hand-picked strings plus 800K random strings, comparing the returned double's raw bits, the `endPtr` offset, and `errno` (including the `ERANGE` paths for `1e512` / `1e-512`).\n\nOne caveat worth flagging, not a defect I introduced: the C `js_fmtexp` writes into a 9-byte `char se[9]` and overruns it for exponents needing 10 digits (e.g. `INT_MAX`), which segfaulted my harness when I probed it. The Rust port reproduces the same out-of-bounds write rather than diverging, per the \"do not fix bugs\" rule; real callers only pass exponents up to about ±330."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a9d0ff26132faef4d (use SendMessage with to: 'a9d0ff26132faef4d', summary: '<5-10 word recap>' to continue this agent)\n<usage>subagent_tokens: 104583\ntool_uses: 22\nduration_ms: 363767</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e867ece2-5670-426a-953e-de496239b763","uuid":"00e7ede5-67aa-439d-8857-5109e5c8eb24","timestamp":"2026-08-22T15:14:28.025Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are translating one C file of the MuJS interpreter into Rust as part of a larger, already-started translation.\n\nWorking directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-translate-iwlyVP/translated_rust\n\nSTEP 1 — Read these files first, fully:\n- TRANSLATION_GUIDE.md   (the rules; follow them exactly)\n- src/jsi.rs             (types/constants/helpers you must use)\n- src/cstd.rs            (libc declarations)\n- src/jserror.rs         (a completed example of the target style)\n- c_src/src/jsdtoa.c     (the file you must translate)\n\nSTEP 2 — Write EXACTLY ONE file: src/jsdtoa.rs   (do not touch any other file)\n\nThis file implements Grisu2 double->shortest-decimal conversion and a portable\nstrtod. It MUST produce bit-identical results to the C code, so translate every\ninteger type exactly:\n  - `uint64_t` -> `u64`, `int32_t`/`int` -> `i32`/`c_int`, `uint32_t` -> `u32`\n  - all shifts/masks/multiplications keep the same widths; use `wrapping_add`,\n    `wrapping_mul`, `wrapping_sub` where C relies on unsigned wraparound\n  - the union/`memcpy` type-punning between `double` and `uint64_t` becomes\n    `f64::to_bits()` / `f64::from_bits()` or `read_unaligned` — pick whichever\n    matches the C code's exact semantics\n  - static const tables become `static ... : [T; N] = [...]` (copy every value)\n\nRequired exported symbols (`#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn`):\n  js_fmtexp   -> `void js_fmtexp(char *p, int e)`\n  js_grisu2   -> `int js_grisu2(double v, char *buffer, int *K)`\n  js_strtod   -> `double js_strtod(const char *as, char **aas)`\n(declared in c_src/src/jsi.h). Everything else in the file is `static` in C and\nmust become a private Rust `unsafe fn` (keep the same names:\ncached_power, digit_gen, double2diy_fp, double_to_uint64, k_comp, minus,\nmultiply, normalize_boundary, normalized_boundaries, and the `diy_fp`\nstruct/typedef).\n\nNote: jsdtoa.c does not use the js_State/exception machinery at all, so no\nerror macros are needed. It may use libc functions (`strto