extern \"C-unwind\"` since its address is taken.\n\n## Verification (differential testing against the real C code)\nI built the C `regexp.c` + `utf.c` and my Rust module into two binaries sharing one identical C driver, and compared byte-for-byte:\n- 31,744 curated cases (patterns × subjects × cflags × eflags): identical\n- 30,000 randomly fuzzed patterns: identical\n- Targeted limit cases (`REG_MAXPROG`, `REG_MAXREC`, `REG_MAXCLASS`, `REG_MAXSPAN`, `REG_MAXSUB`, `REPINF`): identical\n- Custom-allocator path via `js_regcompx`/`js_regfreex` with injected allocation failure at call 0–3, plus `NULL` `sub`, `NULL` `errorp`, `regfree(NULL)`, `regfreex(...,NULL)`: identical, including matching alloc/free counts, which confirms the panic-handler cleanup order matches the C `setjmp` handler.\n- All 15 `die()` error strings were exercised and matched exactly. stderr was empty in every run, confirming the panic-hook suppression works.\n\n## Remaining errors in src/regexp.rs\nNone. `cargo build --release` currently fails only with 15 `E0583 file not found for module` errors for modules not yet written (`jsarray`, `jsbuiltin`, `jsregexp`, `jsstring`, …). The lone grep hit containing the text \"regexp.rs\" is the compiler's help line suggesting creation of `src/jsregexp.rs`, not a diagnostic against my file. To type-check my module past the missing-module resolution failure I used a throwaway crate under `$TMPDIR` (since deleted) containing only `cstd`, `jsi`, `utf`, `utfdata`, `macros`, `regexp` plus a one-line `jsrun` stub for `js_trystackoverflow`; it compiles with zero errors and zero warnings. No files other than `src/regexp.rs` were created or modified in the project.","usage":{"total_tokens":136564,"tool_uses":39,"duration_ms":810723},"uuid":"46031510-8629-4e71-8538-321098b51e5f","session_id":"e867ece2-5670-426a-953e-de496239b763"}
