 {{\n        let mut msgbuf = [0 as c_char; 256];\n        let mut buf = [0 as c_char; 512];\n        snprintf(msgbuf.as_mut_ptr(), 256, $fmt $(, $a)*);\n        snprintf(buf.as_mut_ptr(), 256, c\"%s:%d: \".as_ptr(), (*$J).filename, (*$J).lexline);\n        strcat(buf.as_mut_ptr(), msgbuf.as_ptr());\n        crate::jserror::js_newsyntaxerror($J, buf.as_ptr());\n        crate::jsrun::js_throw($J)\n    }};\n}\n```\n(`snprintf(buf, 256, ...)` — note the C code passes 256 not `sizeof buf`; keep it.)\nUse `jsY_error!(J, c\"...\".as_ptr(), args...)` at every call site. It diverges.\n\nOther details:\n- The keyword table `static const char *keywords[]` must be\n  `static keywords: [*const c_char; N]` — but a `static` array of raw pointers\n  needs `unsafe impl Sync` or a const fn; the simplest correct approach is\n  ```rust\n  static KEYWORDS: [&core::ffi::CStr; N] = [c\"break\", c\"case\", ...];\n  ```\n  and then build the `*const c_char` list where needed. `jsY_findkeyword` calls\n  `jsY_findword(s, keywords, nelem(keywords))`; since `jsY_findword` takes\n  `*const *const c_char`, materialise a local\n  `let list: [*const c_char; N] = core::array::from_fn(|i| KEYWORDS[i].as_ptr());`\n  inside `jsY_findkeyword` (a small stack array, no observable difference), or\n  use a lazily initialised `static mut`. Whichever you choose, the *order* of\n  the keyword strings must be identical to the C table, since jsY_findword\n  does a binary search and returns `TK_BREAK + i`.\n- `J->lexbuf` is `js_LexBuf { text: *mut c_char, len: c_int, cap: c_int }`.\n- `J->lexchar`, `J->lexline`, `J->source`, `J->filename`, `J->text`,\n  `J->number`, `J->lasttoken`, `J->newline` are all fields of `js_State`.\n- utf functions: `use crate::utf::{chartorune, runetochar, runelen, isalpharune};`\n  (`Rune`, `UTFmax`, `Runeerror` come from jsi.rs).\n- `js_realloc`, `js_free`, `js_malloc`, `js_intern` (crate::jsintern),\n  `js_strtod`/`js_grisu2` (crate::jsdtoa), `js_strtol`/`js_stringtofloat`\n  (crate::jsvalue) as needed.\n- The `EOF`-ish sentinel: C uses `#define EOF (-1)` from stdio — define\n  `const EOF: c_int = -1;` locally if used.\n- `strtod`-style number parsing: use exactly the same helper the C code uses.\n- Preserve every `switch` fallthrough explicitly, and the exact order of tests.\n\nSTEP 3 — Verify: run\n`cargo build --release 2>&1 | grep -E \"jslex\\.rs\" | head -60`\nand fix every error attributable to src/jslex.rs. Errors in other modules\n(not written yet) are expected. If the build aborts before type-checking\nbecause other modules are missing (E0583), create a THROWAWAY crate under\n`$TMPDIR` that includes only the modules that exist plus stubs, to type-check\nyour file — and delete it afterwards. Never add stub files to src/.\n\nSTEP 4 — Report the no_mangle symbols defined, line count, and remaining errors."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01ETgG8NADJfipPZJQxv1gzX","session_id":"e867ece2-5670-426a-953e-de496239b763","uuid":"4f0199d7-f5e7-462a-acb8-9946a0ad6db3","timestamp":"2026-08-22T15:23:19.600Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Translate jslex.c to Rust"}
