{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01T7d4pPTpUVVWmY5pWBfvT6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# MuJS C -> Rust translation guide (READ FULLY BEFORE WRITING CODE)\n2\t\n3\tWe are transliterating the C library in `c_src/` into a Rust `cdylib` that\n4\texports the **identical** public ABI and produces **byte-identical** behaviour.\n5\tThis is a *mechanical, line-by-line transliteration* using raw pointers. Do NOT\n6\tredesign, do NOT \"improve\", do NOT fix bugs, do NOT change the order of checks.\n7\t\n8\t`c_src/` is READ-ONLY. Never modify it.\n9\t\n10\t## Crate layout\n11\t\n12\t```\n13\tCargo.toml                 [lib] crate-type = [\"cdylib\"], name = \"mujs\"\n14\tsrc/lib.rs                 module list (already written)\n15\tsrc/cstd.rs                extern \"C\" declarations of libc (already written)\n16\tsrc/macros.rs              error throwing macros (already written)\n17\tsrc/jsi.rs                 all types + constants + try/throw infra (already written)\n18\tsrc/vararg.rs              asm trampolines for the variadic entry points (already written)\n19\tsrc/<file>.rs              one module per c_src/src/<file>.c\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tRead `src/jsi.rs`, `src/cstd.rs`, `src/macros.rs` and (for style reference)\n23\t`src/jsrun.rs` + `src/jserror.rs` before starting. They are the authority for\n24\ttype names, constant names, and helper names.\n25\t\n26\t## Module header boilerplate\n27\t\n28\t```rust\n29\t//! Translation of `c_src/src/<file>.c`\n30\t#![allow(non_snake_case)]\n31\t\n32\tuse crate::cstd::*;\n33\tuse crate::jsi::*;\n34\tuse crate::jsrun::*;\n35\tuse crate::jsvalue::*;\n36\tuse crate::jsproperty::*;\n37\tuse core::ptr::{null, null_mut};\n38\t```\n39\tAdd further `use crate::<module>::*;` as needed. Unused imports are fine\n40\t(warnings are globally allowed in lib.rs).\n41\t\n42\t## Naming and linkage rules\n43\t\n44\t* A **non-static** C function (i.e. it appears in `nm` output of the C object\n45\t  file with `T`) becomes\n46\t  ```rust\n47\t  #[unsafe(no_mangle)]\n48\t  pub unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn <exact_linker_name>(...) -> ... { }\n49\t  ```\n50\t  **The Rust name must be the final linker symbol.** Watch out for the\n51\t  `#define` renames in `src/utf.h` (`chartorune` -> `jsU_chartorune`, ...)\n52\t  and `src/regexp.h` (`regcomp` -> `js_regcomp`, ...). When a header renames a\n53\t  function, name the Rust function after the *renamed* symbol and, if the file\n54\t  uses the short name internally a lot, add\n55\t  `use ... as chartorune;`-style aliases or thin `#[inline]` wrappers.\n56\t* A **static** C function becomes `unsafe fn name(...)` (plain Rust ABI, no\n57\t  `no_mangle`) — but if its address is ever taken (e.g. it is registered as a\n58\t  `js_CFunction`, a `qsort` comparator, or stored in a table) it MUST be\n59\t  `unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn` (still no `no_mangle`).\n60\t* `js_CFunction` implementations (`Ap_push`, `Sp_charAt`, ... anything passed to\n61\t  `js_newcfunction`/`jsB_propf`/`js_newcconstructor`) are\n62\t  `unsafe extern \"C-unwind\" fn(J: *mut js_State)`, referenced as `Some(name)`.\n63\t* Never use plain `extern \"C\"` for functions that can throw — always\n64\t  `extern \"C-unwind\"`, otherwise a JS exception aborts the process.\n65\t* If a symbol must be visible to another Rust module, make it `pub`.\n66\t\n67\t## C types\n68\t\n69\t| C | Rust |\n70\t|---|---|\n71\t| `js_State *` | `*mut js_State` |\n72\t| `int`, `unsigned int` | `c_int`, `c_uint` |\n73\t| `const char *` | `*const c_char` |\n74\t| `char *` | `*mut c_char` |\n75\t| `double` | `f64` |\n76\t| `void *` | `*mut c_void` |\n77\t| `short`/`unsigned short` | `i16` / `u16` (`c_ushort`) |\n78\t| `size_t` | `size_t` (= `usize`) |\n79\t| function pointer typedefs | `js_CFunction`, `js_Finalize`, ... (already `Option<fn>`) |\n80\t\n81\tStruct fields keep their C names, except `type` -> `type_` (`js_Object.type_`,\n82\t`js_Ast.type_`, `js_JumpList.type_`) and `delete` -> `delete`\n83\t(`js_ObjUser.delete` is fine).\n84\t\n85\tUnions: `js_Object.u` is a `#[repr(C)] union js_ObjectU`; write\n86\t`(*obj).u.a.length`, `(*obj).u.c.name`, `(*obj).u.r.flags`, ... exactly like C.\n87\t\n88\t## js_Value\n89\t\n90\t`js_Value` is a 16-byte struct with accessor methods instead of a union\n91\t(the type tag lives in byte 15 and doubles as the NUL terminator of short\n92\tstrings, exactly like C):\n93\t\n94\t| C | Rust |\n95\t|---|---|\n96\t| `v->t.type` | `(*v).ty()` (returns `u32`) |\n97\t| `v->t.type = T` | `(*v).set_ty(T)` |\n98\t| `v->u.number` | `(*v).num()` / `(*v).set_num(x)` |\n99\t| `v->u.boolean` | `(*v).boolean()` / `(*v).set_boolean(x)` |\n100\t| `v->u.litstr` | `(*v).litstr()` / `(*v).set_litstr(p)` |\n101\t| `v->u.memstr` | `(*v).memstr()` / `(*v).set_memstr(p)` |\n102\t| `v->u.object` | `(*v).object()` / `(*v).set_object(p)` |\n103\t| `v->u.shrstr` | `(*v).shrstr()` (const ptr) / `(*v).shrstr_mut()` |\n104\t| `JSV_ISSTRING(v)` | `JSV_ISSTRING(v)` (returns `bool`) |\n105\t| `JSV_TOSTRING(v)` | `JSV_TOSTRING(v)` |\n106\t\n107\tType tags: `JS_TSHRSTR JS_TUNDEFINED JS_TNULL JS_TBOOLEAN JS_TNUMBER\n108\tJS_TLITSTR JS_TMEMSTR JS_TOBJECT` (all `u32`).\n109\tClasses: `JS_COBJECT JS_CARRAY JS_CFUNCTION JS_CSCRIPT JS_CCFUNCTION JS_CERROR\n110\tJS_CBOOLEAN JS_CNUMBER JS_CSTRING JS_CREGEXP JS_CDATE JS_CMATH JS_CJSON\n111\tJS_CARGUMENTS JS_CITERATOR JS_CUSERDATA` (all `c_int`).\n112\t\n113\t## Strings and printf\n114\t\n115\t* C string literals become C literals: `c\"hello\".as_ptr()`.\n116\t* `!strcmp(a,b)` -> `streq(a, b)` (returns `bool`); `strcmp(a,b)` is available\n117\t  directly from `cstd`.\n118\t* Use the real libc `snprintf`/`sprintf`/`printf`/`vsnprintf`/`strtod` from\n119\t  `crate::cstd` for anything format related, so the output bytes are identical.\n120\t  Calling variadic C functions from Rust is allowed and stable.\n121\t  Example: `snprintf(buf.as_mut_ptr(), 32, c\"%.*f\".as_ptr(), width, x);`\n122\t* Fixed C buffers: `let mut buf = [0 as c_char; 32];` then `buf.as_mut_ptr()`.\n123\t  Do **not** use Rust `String`/`format!` for anything observable.\n124\t\n125\t## Integer / float conversion — IMPORTANT\n126\t\n127\tRust's `as` casts from float to integer *saturate*; C's truncate/are UB.\n128\tUse the helpers from `jsi.rs` for every C cast of a `double` to an integer\n129\ttype where the value might be out of range:\n130\t\n131\t* `(int)d` -> `cvt_i32(d)`\n132\t* `(long)d` / `(int64_t)d` -> `cvt_i64(d)`\n133\t* `(unsigned int)d` -> `cvt_u32(d)`\n134\t* `(unsigned short)d` -> `cvt_u16(d)`\n135\t\n136\tInteger→integer casts and integer→float casts are plain `as`.\n137\tSigned shifts that can overflow: mimic C/gcc, e.g. `((a as u32) << n) as i32`.\n138\tInteger arithmetic must not panic: the release profile disables overflow checks,\n139\tbut prefer `wrapping_*` where the C code deliberately overflows.\n140\t\n141\t## Throwing errors\n142\t\n143\tThe 7 variadic error functions are exported from `src/vararg.rs` +\n144\t`src/jserror.rs`; **inside Rust code use the macros** (they format with libc\n145\t`snprintf` into a 256 byte buffer exactly like the C code):\n146\t\n147\t```rust\n148\tjs_typeerror!(J, c\"not a %s\".as_ptr(), tag);\n149\tjs_rangeerror!(J, c\"invalid array length\".as_ptr());\n150\tjs_error!(J, c\"stack underflow!\".as_ptr());\n151\tjs_syntaxerror!(J, c\"unexpected token: %s\".as_ptr(), s);\n152\tjs_referenceerror!(J, ...);  js_evalerror!(J, ...);  js_urierror!(J, ...);\n153\t```\n154\tThey diverge (`-> !`), so they can be used where C relies on `JS_NORETURN`.\n155\t`js_throw(J)` also returns `!`.\n156\t\n157\t## Exception handling (`js_try` / `js_endtry`)\n158\t\n159\tThere is no `setjmp` in Rust. Internal try frames are implemented with\n160\t`catch_unwind`. Translate\n161\t\n162\t```c\n163\tif (js_try(J)) {\n164\t    /* HANDLER */\n165\t}\n166\t/* BODY */\n167\tjs_endtry(J);\n168\t/* TAIL */\n169\t```\n170\t\n171\tas\n172\t\n173\t```rust\n174\tif js_do_try(J, || {\n175\t    /* BODY */\n176\t    js_endtry(J);\n177\t})\n178\t.is_none()\n179\t{\n180\t    /* HANDLER */\n181\t}\n182\t/* TAIL */\n183\t```\n184\t\n185\tNotes:\n186\t* `js_do_try` returns `None` exactly when the C code would have taken the\n187\t  `if (js_try(J))` branch.\n188\t* The closure must call `js_endtry(J)` itself, like the C body does.\n189\t* If the body needs to return a value to the enclosing function, let the\n190\t  closure produce it: `match js_do_try(J, || { ...; js_endtry(J); v }) { None => ..., Some(v) => ... }`.\n191\t* If the C handler does `return x;`, put that `return x;` in the `is_none()`\n192\t  block. If it does `js_throw(J)`, put `js_throw(J)` there.\n193\t* Locals that the C code declares `volatile` because they are read in the\n194\t  handler must be shared with the closure through a raw pointer:\n195\t  ```rust\n196\t  let mut sab: *mut c_char = null_mut();\n197\t  let sabp = &mut sab as *mut *mut c_char;\n198\t  if js_do_try(J, || { *sabp = ...; js_endtry(J); }).is_none() { ... }\n199\t  ```\n200\t\n201\t## Control flow translation patterns\n202\t\n203\t* `goto label;` at the end of a function (`readonly:`, `dontconf:`, ...) ->\n204\t  labelled block:\n205\t  ```rust\n206\t  'readonly: {\n207\t      ...\n208\t      break 'readonly;   // was: goto readonly\n209\t      ...\n210\t      return;            // normal exit before the label\n211\t  }\n212\t  /* readonly: */\n213\t  ...\n214\t  ```\n215\t* `for (i = 0; i < n; ++i)` -> `let mut i = 0; while i < n { ...; i += 1; }`\n216\t  (careful with `continue` — the increment must still happen).\n217\t* `do { } while (cond);` -> `loop { ...; if !(cond) { break; } }`\n218\t* `switch` -> `match` on the value with `_ => {}` for `default`. C fallthrough\n219\t  must be written out explicitly. Remember `break` inside a C `switch` is not a\n220\t  loop break.\n221\t* C's `,` operator / assignment-as-expression must be unfolded into statements.\n222\t* `p[i]` on a raw pointer -> `*p.offset(i as isize)`.\n22