e (`m.sub[x]`, `m.sub[k]`) I used a raw-pointer accessor `m_sub(m, i) -> *mut ResubSpan` instead of Rust array indexing, so the `for (x = 0; m.sub[x].sp; ++x)` loop in `Sp_replace_regexp` reads past `REG_MAXSUB` exactly like C rather than panicking on a bounds check.\n- **`Sp_substring_imp`:** C reads `p` in the `js_try` handler while `p` is *uninitialized* at that point (it is not `volatile`, and the handler can fire from inside `js_malloc`). I initialize the shared pointer to `null_mut()`, which makes `js_free` a no-op instead of freeing garbage.\n- **`goto loop` / `goto end`** in `Sp_replace_regexp` became a single `loop { ... }` with `continue` for `goto loop` and `break` for both `goto end` and the fallthrough; `s`/`n` are declared outside so the post-label `js_puts(J, &sb, s + n)` sees the right values.\n- **`case 0: --r; /* fallthrough */`** in both `$`-substitution switches is written out explicitly (`0 =>` arm does the `--r` then duplicates the `'$'` output).\n- **`istrim(*s)`** takes `*s as c_int` on a signed `c_char`, preserving the C sign-extension (so the `0xA0`/`0xFEFF`/`0x2028` comparisons can never match — the original behaviour).\n- `volatile` locals (`out`, `dst`, `s`, `sb`, plus the address-taken `sb`/`m`) are shared with the `js_do_try` closures through raw pointers per the guide's pattern.","usage":{"total_tokens":127080,"tool_uses":25,"duration_ms":456984},"uuid":"81fbc7d3-3062-4725-910b-9aeb1914dfee","session_id":"e867ece2-5670-426a-953e-de496239b763"}
